     1	# Tessera — Phase 0 Pre-Registration
     2	
     3	> **What this document is.** A dated, GPG-signed, OpenTimestamps-anchored
     4	> declaration of intent, made *before* the system it describes is built. Its
     5	> credibility comes from the fact that it cannot be backdated: the Bitcoin
     6	> anchor on the commit that introduces it fixes its existence in time. The
     7	> build journal that follows will be measured *against* this document —
     8	> including, honestly, where reality diverged from it.
     9	>
    10	> **Why pre-register at all.** Anyone can write a flattering after-the-fact
    11	> story. Pre-registration converts the build log from a highlight reel into
    12	> evidence: the hypotheses, success criteria, and design commitments are on
    13	> record before the outcomes are known, so the reader can check the work
    14	> instead of trusting the narrator.
    15	
    16	---
    17	
    18	## 0. Provenance of this document
    19	
    20	- **Author:** Tony Mason (architecture, judgment, all decisions herein).
    21	- **AI collaborator:** Tyst (`tessera@wamason.com`) — mechanics, drafting,
    22	  pushback. Division of labor is explicit and recorded; see §6.
    23	- **Integrity:** this commit is signed by the Tyst key (ed25519,
    24	  `2B9C F308 ...42C7 3835`) and anchored via OpenTimestamps. The signing key
    25	  is the WHO layer; the OTS anchor is the WHEN layer.
    26	
    27	An AWS native attestation service modeled on our work in Willay: a robust
    28	demonstration of building, validating, observing, and deploying a
    29	beta-quality attestation service.
    30	
    31	---
    32	
    33	## 1. Objective and the honest ranking
    34	
    35	The function of an attestation service is to provide a neutral, third
    36	party's cryptographic binding to a package of information delivered to
    37	it.  Attestation in this usage is similar to SSL certificates that say
    38	"this attestation says that the party in control of a website has
    39	demonstrated this control and the following certificate validates it."
    40	
    41	The distinction here is that a Tessera attestation is an attestation of
    42	information at a fixed point in time, while an SSL certificate is an
    43	attestation of website ownership with a fixed expiration.
    44	
    45	Why would you use an attestation:
    46	
    47	1. to demonstrate a tamper-resistant pre-registration, such as a hypothesis,
    48	   an experimental design, and a predicted outcome.
    49	2. to record the findings of one or more third parties, such as an
    50	   audit, regulatory policy, ontology, or citation.  Note: this is a
    51	   suggested but not exhaustive list.
    52	3. to record agreements between parties, such as a contract, and
    53	   signatures of parties to that contract, similar to the way a notary validates
    54	   documents.
    55	
    56	The distinction here is that this attestation is over a _digital artifact_.  Thus,
    57	for example, the SHA256 hash value of a document, or a blockchain address, or
    58	some other unique identifier, might be part of the digital artifact.
    59	
    60	This service provides a cryptographic signature and timestamp over the digital artifact
    61	and that attestation receipt permits future parties to verify the attestation.
    62	The design is done in such a way that the receipt is verifiable independent
    63	of the attestor, which ensures the ability of parties in the future to validate
    64	the signed artifact.
    65	
    66	**The honest ranking.** Two objectives, strictly ranked, and the ranking is
    67	the discipline:
    68	
    69	1. **Primary — the demonstration.** Close the operational-envelope gap (§7):
    70	   show that I can build, validate, observe, secure, and deploy an attestation
    71	   service to a beta-quality bar, with the engineering rigor visible in the
    72	   repository itself. This is the objective. It succeeds or fails on its own,
    73	   independent of whether anyone ever pays for the service.
    74	
    75	2. **Secondary — possible commercial service (upside, not premise).** Tessera
    76	   may become a viable attestation-as-a-service. If it does, good — but the
    77	   revenue-bearing pieces (billing/payment integration, PCI *certification*,
    78	   customer acquisition, scaling for real traffic) are **deferred until
    79	   validated paying demand exists**, and built no sooner. The product dream
    80	   does not expand the demonstration's scope. Note the distinction the primary
    81	   objective *does* honor: Tessera is architected **to the PCI standard**
    82	   (minimal cardholder-data environment, segmentation, key custody) as a matter
    83	   of engineering discipline — designing to the bar is demonstration; obtaining
    84	   the certificate is product, and waits.
    85	
    86	**Credibility line (non-negotiable):** every architecture decision here is
    87	the human author (Tony) in collaboration with AI agents. The purpose of
    88	the AI agents is to accelerate the typing, push back on my decisions, clarify
    89	them, but not to _make_ those decisions without consultation.
    90	
    91	---
    92	
    93	## 2. Hypotheses (falsifiable, dated)
    94	
    95	A key aspect of designing this project is to identify the anticipated
    96	criteria for evaluating the project itself.
    97	
    98	The work is decomposed into dependency-ordered bands. The bands matter because
    99	they have different risk profiles, and the hypotheses are stated per band rather
   100	than lumped into one figure. **Band 0 precedes all of them:**
   101	
   102	0. **Band 0 — the formal foundation (H0).** Before any code, the central
   103	   guarantee is established by a machine-checked formal model and proof (see
   104	   H0). This is formal-methods work, not software engineering, and it gates
   105	   everything: if the guarantee cannot be proven, there is nothing worth
   106	   building. No time bound — a rushed proof is worse than none — and the
   107	   Phase 1 / H1b clock does not start until Band 0 is resolved.
   108	1. **Band 1 — cloud-independent core.** The cryptographic guarantees, proven
   109	   on a laptop with no AWS and no LocalStack. This is the invariant the later
   110	   bands must not break. It has no irreducible wall-clock latency in its test
   111	   path (OTS *confirmation* latency is deferred to band 3; band 1 tests use
   112	   stamped-not-yet-confirmed proofs). Throughput-bound, fast.
   113	2. **Band 2 — local vertical slice.** The same guarantees delivered through
   114	   the LocalStack-emulated pipeline (S3/DynamoDB/SQS/Lambda/KMS), sustaining
   115	   a steady issue rate. Cloud-*shaped* but free and local.
   116	3. **Band 3 — real cloud.** The operated service on actual AWS: the attack
   117	   surface, real KMS, observability, adversarial attack, cost/capacity claims.
   118	   Wall-clock-bound (terraform apply, GuardDuty population, OTS confirmation),
   119	   slow and externally gated.
   120	
   121	- **H0 — the foundational claim (gates the entire project).** Tessera's central
   122	  guarantee — that altering any part of a signed attestation package requires
   123	  solving a computationally hard problem — is established by *formal proof of a
   124	  machine-checked model*, with one honestly-declared gap between model and code.
   125	  This is not software engineering; it is the precondition for Tessera being a
   126	  *commercial* offering rather than a demonstration of confidence. A party
   127	  relying on an attestation in adjudication must be able to *depend* on the
   128	  guarantee, not trust an assertion of it.
   129	
   130	  *Two layers, kept distinct (so the claim cannot be accused of formal-methods
   131	  theater):*
   132	  - **Layer 1 — symbolic composition invariant (what the checker proves).**
   133	    Assuming *idealized* signatures, hashes, canonicalization, and temporal
   134	    anchors, a machine-checked model (TLA+ and/or Lean4) establishes that the
   135	    verifier accepts only packages satisfying the intended integrity and
   136	    ordering properties — e.g. "no transition leads to an accepted receipt over
   137	    altered bytes," "ordering/replay conditions hold." This is exactly what
   138	    state-machine and proof tooling is good at.
   139	  - **Layer 2 — computational assumptions (what is depended on, not proven).**
   140	    The idealized primitives are justified by *external* assumptions we cite,
   141	    not discharge: signature unforgeability, SHA-256 collision/preimage
   142	    resistance (only as actually needed), and Bitcoin/OpenTimestamps timestamp
   143	    assumptions. The cryptographic claim "altering any part requires solving a
   144	    computationally hard problem" holds *under these stated assumptions and an
   145	    explicit adversary model* — it is not proven from nothing.
   146	
   147	  *The verification chain, by trust source:*
   148	  1. **A machine-checked formal model** of the construction (Layer 1 above).
   149	     The checker establishes that *the model* has the claimed properties — but
   150	     **not** that the model is the *right* model. Model fidelity to intent is a
   151	     human judgment, not a checkable one. What is proven is **our own
   152	     composition** (Layer 1) under the cited primitive assumptions (Layer 2);
   153	     the document states crisply which is which.
   154	  2. **An agreement gate:** author and AI must each independently agree the
   155	     model faithfully captures the claim before it is handed onward — a
   156	     cross-model convergence, not a single nod, to catch "the checker proved the
   157	     wrong model."
   158	  3. **An informal written proof** (not required for the proof obligation —
   159	     the checked model carries that — but required as the H2 evidence for the
   160	     formal core). A passing checker is identical whether the human owns the
   161	     formalism or prompted an AI into it; the written, defend-it-cold prose is
   162	     the artifact that demonstrates the *author's understanding* of the core,
   163	     which is the primary objective (§1) and the deskilling defense (§6) at the
   164	     spot it is hardest and most tempting to skip.
   165	
   166	  *Human external review is NOT a resolution requirement — deliberately.* The
   167	  gate is **machine-checked** (free, deterministic, repeatable, in CI, in our
   168	  control). Requiring an *independent human* formal-methods reviewer would set
   169	  the bar where it cannot be met under the pre-registered no-spend constraint
   170	  (H3): a competent reviewer costs money, so a human-review gate would either
   171	  stall the project indefinitely in Band 0 or force spend we committed against.
   172	  Therefore human external review is an **optional enhancement, gated on a
   173	  revenue stream (the secondary objective, §1)**. Its *absence is a declared
   174	  residual risk:* a machine-checked proof and multi-model agreement reduce the
   175	  probability of an undetected flaw but do not eliminate it — no review,
   176	  machine or human, guarantees a future party will not find a hole. That
   177	  residual risk is accepted and disclosed, not concealed. (Earlier drafts made
   178	  human external review a hard gate; that was an over-tightening, removed.)
   179	
   180	  *The declared gap (what is attested, not proven):* the chain proves *the
   181	  model* sound. It does **not** prove that *the code implements the model* —
   182	  that link is *attested, not proven*. When the code deviates from the model,
   183	  that is a *bug*, hunted by the H1a adversarial suite — the ordinary
   184	  spec-to-implementation relationship, stated plainly rather than concealed.
   185	  Declaring this gap is the Tessera ethos applied to Tessera: declare what you
   186	  cannot see.
   187	
   188	  *Resolved when:* the machine-checked model (1) holds under its stated
   189	  assumptions, the agreement gate (2) has passed, and the informal written
   190	  proof (3) is in the repository, with the model-to-code gap documented. Human
   191	  external review is not required for resolution (see above); if later obtained
   192	  under a revenue stream, its outcome is recorded. *Falsified if:* no such proof
   193	  can be produced, or
   194	  it shows the guarantee fails under realistic assumptions — **the project does
   195	  not proceed to Phase 1 as designed.** The H1b clock does not start until H0
   196	  is resolved.
   197	
   198	- **H1a (band 1 — crypto core):** The cloud-independent cryptographic core is
   199	  frozen *before* any LocalStack work begins, with adversarial evidence that
   200	  the implementation conforms to the H0 model. (Tests do not *prove*
   201	  correctness — H0 is the proof gate; H1a is *implementation evidence* that the
   202	  code rejects the enumerated attacks and conforms to the model's expected
   203	  verifier behavior.) *Falsified if:* there is not a public, adversarially-
   204	  authored red-bar suite that, for each attack family — forge, tamper,
   205	  backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature — and each degradation scenario,
   206	  shows the verifier emits the **correct explicit verification state** (see
   207	  §4.6): `VALID_STRICT`, `VALID_DEGRADED(policy)`, `INVALID`, or `UNVERIFIABLE`.
   208	  Emitting the *right* state for every case is the criterion — including the
   209	  fail-open trap of returning `VALID` where the honest answer is `UNVERIFIABLE`.
   210	- **H1b (band 2 — vertical slice speed):** A local vertical slice (issue →
   211	  verify → chain a receipt, all on LocalStack) sustaining **1 attestation/second**
   212	  lands within **7 calendar days** of the Band 1 freeze. The 1/sec figure is a
   213	  *functional throughput* check (the pipeline sustains a steady rate without
   214	  deadlock or chain corruption), explicitly **not** a real-infrastructure
   215	  performance claim — LocalStack's performance characteristics are not AWS's.
   216	  *Clock (as a state machine, no ambiguity):*
   217	  H0 resolved → Phase 1a implements the cloud-independent crypto core →
   218	  **H1a freeze commit** (dated, signed; this both closes H1a and opens the H1b
   219	  window) → Phase 1b builds the LocalStack slice → **H1b evidence commit** must
   220	  land within 7 calendar days of the H1a freeze commit. Both endpoints live in
   221	  the anchored git history; elapsed time is auditable from the timestamps, not
   222	  asserted from memory.
   223	  *Falsified if:* no H1b evidence commit demonstrating 1/sec sustained on
   224	  LocalStack lands within 7 calendar days of the H1a freeze commit.
   225	- **H2 (division of labor):** The architecture decisions are demonstrably
   226	  mine; AI handled mechanics. *Falsified if:* the multi-model review panel (the
   227	  same ≥3 unrelated LLM models that adversarially review security in §5, here
   228	  tasked to interrogate architecture ownership) finds a load-bearing decision I
   229	  cannot justify without reconstructing it from the AI's reasoning. The panel is a
   230	  proxy for the only reviewer who matters: someone reading the repository to
   231	  see what was actually done.
   232	- **H3 (cost discipline):** Three separate numbers, so none can be trivially
   233	  contradicted by the pricing page:
   234	  - **demo-cycle variable cost under $25** — a complete cycle (`terraform apply`
   235	    → run the full red-bar + load suite → `terraform destroy`) at standard
   236	    on-demand pricing;
   237	  - **persistent monthly footprint under $5/month** — the intentional always-on
   238	    pieces (each customer-managed KMS key ~$1/mo, a Route 53 hosted zone
   239	    ~$0.50/mo, the ledger's S3 bucket) are low single-digit dollars, *not*
   240	    "cents";
   241	  - **idle compute exactly $0** — nothing *runs* between demonstrations (no
   242	    Fargate task, idle EC2, or provisioned capacity).
   243	
   244	  Cost is measured against *standard* pricing, not free-tier: free-tier credits
   245	  may cover actual spend, but H3 evidences cost *governance*, not subsidy.
   246	  *Falsified if:* a measured demo cycle exceeds $25 at standard rates, the
   247	  persistent footprint exceeds $5/month, or any idle compute persists between
   248	  demonstrations.
