   251	
   252	## 3. Architecture decisions (the part that is genuinely yours)
   253	
   254	### 3.1 The signing-architecture decision — KMS vs. GPG vs. dual
   255	
   256	The decision space, stated neutrally before the decision and its reasoning:
   257	
   258	- **Willay's model (A):** local GPG signs; verification needs only the
   259	  pinned fingerprint and the bundle. Maximally portable, zero infrastructure.
   260	  Rejected for Tessera because it exercises no cloud-native services — it's
   261	  "Willay with extra overhead."
   262	- **KMS-native (B):** KMS signs the cloud receipt. This exercises real
   263	  cloud-native key custody, and current KMS supports offline-verifiable
   264	  asymmetric signatures — including `ECC_NIST_EDWARDS25519` (Ed25519) and
   265	  post-quantum `ML-DSA` (FIPS 204) key specs; the private key remains in KMS
   266	  while the public key downloads via `GetPublicKey` for verification outside
   267	  AWS. B is therefore **technically viable** — the earlier belief that KMS
   268	  could not sign Ed25519 was wrong (corrected against current AWS docs). It is
   269	  rejected as the *sole* trust mechanism not because KMS is incapable, but
   270	  because it concentrates issuer custody, AWS account lifecycle, key-deletion
   271	  risk, IAM/policy risk, and public-key-provenance risk in **one operational
   272	  domain**.
   273	- **Dual-attestation (C), the chosen path:** KMS signs the cloud receipt to
   274	  demonstrate cloud-native custody, **and** local GPG counter-signs to preserve
   275	  a portable, independent WHO layer (Willay's). Public keys are published via
   276	  DNS (`wamason.com`, DNSSEC), repositories, and the bundle. The justification
   277	  is **not** that KMS is incapable; it is that a long-lived attestation should
   278	  not rest on a single operational trust domain.
   279	
   280	**Decision: C.**
   281	
   282	**Why C, defended cold.** The threat model for an attestation is not "is this
   283	signature valid today" — it is "will this signature adjudicate in fifty years,
   284	when the issuer may be gone." B is technically capable (it can even produce
   285	offline-verifiable Ed25519 or post-quantum signatures), but it places the
   286	entire trust story in **one operational domain**: AWS account lifecycle, KMS
   287	key-deletion policy, IAM, and the provenance of the published public key all
   288	sit together. If that domain lapses — the account closes, the key is deleted,
   289	the policy changes, the published key's provenance becomes unclear — the
   290	attestation's verifiability is at risk, all at once, for correlated reasons.
   291	
   292	C signs the same canonical bytes with two keys from *independent failure
   293	domains* — KMS (cloud-native custody) and local GPG (Willay's portable WHO
   294	layer). They fail for *uncorrelated* reasons (AWS account/policy lapse vs. local
   295	key disclosure), so the attestation's durability does not rest on any single
   296	domain surviving. **Not replicating is what concentrates the risk**; the cost of
   297	C over B is essentially nil, and the benefit is that attestations stand up to
   298	adjudication even if one trust domain disappears entirely.
   299	
   300	**Key distribution is a separate axis from dual-signing.** Public keys are not
   301	secret; they are published redundantly — via DNS (`wamason.com`, DNSSEC), in
   302	public repositories, and in the attestation bundle itself. This defends against
   303	any single distribution channel expiring. Note this redundancy could be applied
   304	to B as well; it is *not* what justifies C. What only C provides is two
   305	independent signatures whose validity does not share a failure domain.
   306	
   307	**Verification policy lives with the verifier, not frozen into the receipt.**
   308	The attestation carries a *set* of signatures (a JSON map of key-type →
   309	signature), not an AND/OR rule. The default reference verifier requires **all
   310	issue-time signatures to verify** — the strong guarantee a naive holder gets
   311	without having to understand the policy.
   312	
   313	This fail-closed default is not a fragility to apologize for; it is the system
   314	refusing to lie. If one signature can no longer be verified, we will **not**
   315	let a naive holder assume the attestation is still fully valid. A verifier
   316	*may* override and accept a lower bar — but the act of overriding is itself an
   317	informed-consent step: by relaxing the requirement, the verifier has
   318	demonstrated they understand the tradeoff and have knowingly accepted a weaker
   319	guarantee, thereby changing their own self-label from "naive" to "not naive."
   320	The objection "but naive users will be misled by a relaxed check" therefore
   321	cannot land: the category it worries about cannot exist, because relaxing the
   322	check is precisely the act that exits naivety. (Long-term, the wrapping model
   323	below lets fresh assurance be re-anchored *before* old algorithms fail, so a
   324	fully-strict verification can remain available across decades — but that is
   325	additional assurance, not a rescue this default requires.)
   326	
   327	That argument is the *justification*; the *mechanism* that implements and tests
   328	it is an explicit set of verifier states (this is what makes the policy
   329	auditable rather than rhetorical, and it is the H1a acceptance criterion —
   330	§4.6):
   331	- `VALID_STRICT` — all issue-time signatures verify (the fail-closed default);
   332	- `VALID_DEGRADED(policy=...)` — accepted under an explicit, recorded weaker
   333	  policy the verifier chose (the informed-consent override);
   334	- `INVALID` — a required check failed;
   335	- `UNVERIFIABLE` — a check could not be performed (missing key, unsupported
   336	  algorithm, expired trust root). Critically, this is **never** silently
   337	  promoted to `VALID`.
   338	
   339	**Temporal upgrade by wrapping, not replacement.** Cryptographic strength
   340	decays; post-quantum standards are still in flux, and the right time to choose
   341	a PQ algorithm is when a customer needs that guarantee — it will be a
   342	*different* key algorithm, a real architectural choice encoded per-receipt.
   343	Rather than re-sign old payloads (which would require the original
   344	canonicalization to remain byte-identical forever), a **superseding
   345	attestation wraps the original package** and signs *that* with the new-algorithm
   346	key. The inner attestation's bytes, signatures, and OTS proof are immutable;
   347	the wrapper is a new layer with its own time-anchor. This (a) makes the
   348	upsigning *visible and ordered* rather than a silent mutation, (b) lets a
   349	customer add PQ assurance *while the original keys are still trusted* — the
   350	window in which bootstrapping new trust is actually sound — and (c) dissolves
   351	the canonicalization-freeze problem into per-layer versioning: each layer
   352	records which canonical form it used. Wrapping is a *paid* operation; nesting
   353	depth is therefore bounded by willingness to pay (as PKI key sprawl is bounded
   354	by storage budget), not by a protocol cap. It is for rare, high-value
   355	supersession, not routine.
   356	
   357	**What the wrapper attests.** The base service wrapper attests *integrity of
   358	the inner object* — "this 2026 package existed and is bit-for-bit unaltered,
   359	re-anchored under a 2030 algorithm." It commits to the inner package's **bytes**,
   360	**not** to the inner receipt's *verification result* at wrap time: the wrapper
   361	does not assert "the inner receipt was VALID_STRICT when wrapped," because that
   362	would be a form of re-evaluation. The inner receipt's validity stands on its own
   363	signatures, verified independently whenever a relying party checks it. It does
   364	**not** re-evaluate the original claim. Semantic re-attestation (a genuinely new inspection over the old
   365	package, with its own losses and open questions) is a separate, priced business
   366	offering, and — honestly declared — *may not be available years later*, because
   367	the evaluator, context, and resolvable evidence degrade over time. (An expert
   368	report written in 2021 can be updated in 2031, but not for free, and perhaps
   369	not at all.)
   370	
   371	**The temporal anchor is an independent trust dimension that crypto advances
   372	do not touch.** The *intended* argument: even total signature compromise should
   373	not let a forger rewrite the *past*, because forging a Tessera attestation would
   374	require breaking the signature algorithm(s) **and** producing a valid
   375	OTS/Bitcoin anchor at the original time — i.e., forging Bitcoin's proof-of-work
   376	history. Length is bound into the canonical bytes every signature commits to, so
   377	length-extension / boundary-ambiguity attacks must also defeat both keys *and*
   378	the anchor.
   379	
   380	**This argument is not yet proven, and that gap gates the project (H0).** The
   381	paragraph above states the guarantee we *believe* holds; it is an assertion, not
   382	a formal result. Establishing it — a threat model, a machine-checked formal
   383	model of the composition, and a proof that the model has the claimed invariants
   384	— is **Band 0**, the precondition for building anything. Asserting it here
   385	without proof would be exactly the theater this project exists to refute. See
   386	H0.
   387	
   388	**Four distinct threat classes (do not conflate them).** The cryptographic
   389	story above defends two of these; the other two need *operational* controls,
   390	and saying so plainly is part of declaring what we cannot see:
   391	- **Cryptographic forgery** — an attacker produces an accepted receipt without
   392	  authorized signing authority. *Mitigation:* the dual-signature + anchor
   393	  construction (H0).
   394	- **Tampering** — an attacker alters an issued package after the fact.
   395	  *Mitigation:* same construction; the signatures and anchor break.
   396	- **Misissuance** — an *authorized or compromised issuer path* signs content it
   397	  should not. Dual signatures do **not** prevent this if the attacker can drive
   398	  both signers through the legitimate issuance path. *Mitigation:* authorization
   399	  controls and audit logs, **not** cryptography. Named here so it is not
   400	  mistaken for something the signing construction solves.
   401	- **Key compromise** — an attacker obtains signing capability outside normal
   402	  policy. *Mitigation:* KMS/GPG domain separation (§3.1) reduces correlated
   403	  compromise; OpenTimestamps constrains backdating; the §4.2 threat model and
   404	  TTL bound the local-key blast radius.
   405	
   406	**Open question (declared, deferred):** a *quantitative* forgery-cost analysis
   407	of the combined construction (signature-set + length-bound canonicalization +
   408	OTS anchor) is not yet done. The argument above is qualitative
   409	(independent-failure-domains); the quantitative bound is unproven. Deferring it
   410	is honest — and the scenario that would make it worth doing (a customer with
   411	many supersessions) is itself the revenue signal that funds the analysis.
   412	
   413	**Hard invariants this decision incurs (see §4):** the `canonical(payload) →
   414	bytes` function is the true root of trust and must be frozen and explicitly
   415	versioned; the verification path must depend on *zero* service-side state.
   416	
   417	### 3.2 The shared-core / forked-edge boundary
   418	
   419	The prior spec proposed reusing Willay's `canonical.py`, signing, models,
   420	evaluators, and ledger logic unchanged, forking only the cloud edges (compute,
   421	key custody, storage, queue), with clean extractability as a goal.
   422	
   423	**Decision: the shared-core / forked-edge boundary is not a design priority
   424	for Tessera.** Tessera is a demonstration of *building and operating the
   425	service*, not a reusable, detachable, re-deployable library — that
   426	extractability goal belongs to Willay, not here. Code is reused from Willay
   427	where convenient, but clean separation is not an architectural commitment, and
   428	no effort is spent making the core independently redeployable. This is a
   429	deliberate *narrowing* of scope: optimizing for extractability we do not need
   430	would be wasted work.
   431	
   432	(The one piece of Willay's core that *is* load-bearing — and therefore is a
   433	hard invariant, see §4 — is the canonicalization function, because the
   434	wrap/upgrade model in §3.1 depends on it being frozen and versioned. That is a
   435	property of the bytes, not of code extractability.)
   436	
   437	---
   438	
   439	## 4. Hard invariants
   440	
   441	The five standard NFRs (security, reliability, observability, scalability,
   442	usability) are addressed operationally in §5 and the design spec. The items
   443	below are *hard invariants* — not aspirations but rules with mechanical
   444	enforcement, committed to before any implementation. They are the lines that,
   445	if crossed, mean the system has failed regardless of what else works.
   446	
   447	### 4.1 Secure observability (hard rule)
   448	Key material is **unloggable by construction** — never logged, not private
   449	keys, not passphrases, not "redacted-but-present," not in a debug branch, not
   450	as a joke. Enforced *mechanically*: structured logging that cannot serialize
   451	key types, plus a CI secret-scan gate that fails the build if signing material
   452	reaches a log sink. Discipline is not the control; the type system and CI are.
   453	
   454	### 4.2 Signing-key threat model (explicit, accepted)
   455	The commit-signing key (Tyst, ed25519, `...42C73835`) is **passphraseless**
   456	with a **6-month TTL** (expires 2026-12-07), residing on a firewalled
   457	workstation. **Threat accepted:** host compromise lets an attacker sign as
   458	the tessera committer. **Mitigation:** short TTL + revocation certificate
   459	(already generated). **Deferred (deliberately, not by oversight):**
   460	higher-assurance signing (enclave/secure sandbox so the key never touches an
   461	internet-facing system) until there is real demand for it. This is a defensible
   462	demonstration-project tradeoff; it would NOT be acceptable for a production
   463	root-of-trust, and the journal should say so.
   464	
   465	**TTL note.** 6 months is acceptable *for the demonstration* — a passphraseless
   466	key on a firewalled box facing a low-probability, bounded-blast-radius threat.
   467	**Trigger to revisit:** if anyone relies on issued attestations, shorten the
   468	TTL toward monthly *and* move to the deferred enclave/HSM model so
   469	passphraseless-ness stops being acceptable at all.
   470	
   471	**Hardware token (e.g. YubiKey) — considered, deferred.** A hardware token for
   472	the local GPG layer would close the passphraseless vulnerability and read well
   473	on a CV. It is declined *for now*, for two reasons that are on-thesis rather
   474	than convenience: (1) it reintroduces interactive touch-to-sign, defeating the
   475	headless, non-interactive signing the project deliberately chose; (2) it
   476	concentrates local-layer custody in a single hardware token — a step *toward*
   477	the single-domain concentration §3.1 rejects, not away from it. It sits in the
   478	same deferred enclave/HSM tier, gated on real reliance, not adopted as security
   479	theater.
   480	
   481	**Security review (flagged).** This section is the one most worth an
   482	independent adversarial pass. Per the project's method (§6), that review is a
   483	*different model's* job, not the model that authored this — it is a tracked
   484	item, not a claim of completeness here.
   485	
   486	### 4.3 Canonicalization is frozen and versioned (hard rule)
   487	`canonical(payload) → bytes` is the true root of trust — more than any key.
   488	The wrap/upgrade model (§3.1) requires that a signature added years later
   489	commit to bytes reconstructible exactly as they were at issue time. Therefore
   490	the canonical form is **frozen and explicitly versioned**: it never changes
   491	silently; any change is a *new, versioned* canonical form, and every receipt
   492	records which version it used. Length is bound into the canonical bytes (not a
   493	side field), so the encoding is **boundary-unambiguous** by construction (a
   494	parser cannot disagree about where the payload ends).
   495	
   496	**Bind to an existing deterministic standard, not a hand-rolled function.**
   497	Canonicalization is the graveyard of cryptographic implementations: differing
   498	language runtimes disagree on whitespace, key ordering, number precision, and
   499	Unicode escaping, which silently breaks cross-platform verification. The
   500	canonical form binds to **RFC 8785 (JSON Canonicalization Scheme, JCS)** rather
   501	than a bespoke `canonical()`. JCS's one sharp edge — numbers must be exactly
   502	representable as IEEE 754 doubles — is handled by an explicit invariant: **any
   503	value not exactly a double (hashes, large/256-bit integers, blockchain
   504	addresses, opaque identifiers) is string-encoded, never a JSON number.** (JCS is
   505	an Informational RFC, not IETF standards-track — cited as the deterministic
   506	serialization we adopt, with the precision rule above closing its gap.)
   507	
   508	Operational commitments that make this checkable, not merely asserted:
   509	1. canonicalization **test vectors** are part of the public verifier
   510	   conformance suite;
   511	2. every canonicalization **version has byte-for-byte golden examples**;
   512	3. **wrappers record both inner and outer** canonicalization versions.
   513	
   514	(We claim boundary-unambiguity, which is what the length-binding buys. We do
   515	*not* make a blanket "length-extension-resistant" claim — that is
   516	construction-specific and would invite cryptographic nitpicking we have not
   517	earned; the relevant property here is unambiguous framing, proven in Band 0.)
   518	
   519	### 4.4 Verification path depends on zero service-side state (hard rule)
   520	An attestation must verify with the service **dead**. The verification path
   521	touches no database, no live service, no cloud reachability. The operational
   522	database exists to *run* the service (who may request an attestation, rate
   523	limits, keys, later billing) and is **never** on the path that *verifies* one.
   524	If verifying ever requires service-side state, the verification-independence
   525	guarantee is broken.
   526	
   527	**DNS and repositories are *discovery* mechanisms, not *verification*
   528	dependencies.** A public key sitting in the bundle proves little by itself — the
   529	future verifier needs to know that key was an *authorized issuer key at the
   530	relevant time*. Therefore the bundle carries an **issuer-key manifest**: the
   531	issuer public keys (or references) plus *timestamped evidence* that those keys
   532	were authorized issuer keys at issue time, the manifest itself anchored. The
   533	future verifier then has an archived trust path inside the bundle, rather than
   534	needing live DNS or a reachable repository. DNS/repo remain useful for *finding*
   535	keys; they are not *required* to *verify*.
   536	
   537	**Revocation is time-relative.** A key revoked *after* issue does not retroactively
   538	invalidate attestations made *while it was authorized* — the manifest's
   539	timestamped authorization evidence, anchored, establishes the key was valid *at
   540	issue time*, and an attestation signed then remains `VALID_STRICT`. The naive
   541	reading ("the key is revoked now, therefore the receipt is invalid") is wrong and
   542	is exactly the kind of error the verifier-state logic must get right: revocation
   543	*after* the anchored issue time is irrelevant to that receipt; revocation
   544	*effective before or at* issue time means the signature was never authorized
   545	(`INVALID`). This is precisely why the temporal anchor (WHEN) is load-bearing
   546	alongside the signature (WHO).
   547	
   548	### 4.5 Adversarial tests are authored by a different model (hard rule)
   549	The red-bar suite's attacks are authored by a *different* model (Codex) than
   550	the one that wrote the implementation, so the implementer cannot unconsciously
   551	shape attacks to what the code survives. This is not perfect (shared
   552	training-distribution blind spots remain) but is a real, defensible separation,
   553	and stronger than self-authored tests. Test/code separation is CI-enforced.
   554	
   555	### 4.6 The verifier returns an explicit typed state, never a bare boolean (hard rule)
   556	Verification yields one of `VALID_STRICT`, `VALID_DEGRADED(policy=...)`,
   557	`INVALID`, or `UNVERIFIABLE` (§3.1) — a typed result, never a bare `true`/`false`.
   558	A boolean collapses the load-bearing distinction between "this failed a check"
   559	(`INVALID`) and "this check could not be performed" (`UNVERIFIABLE`), which is
   560	exactly where fail-open bugs hide. The typed state is what makes the fail-closed
   561	policy *auditable and testable* rather than rhetorical, and it is the form H1a's
   562	acceptance criteria assert against.
   563	
   564	### 4.7 Review recency is itself attested (hard rule)
   565	This pre-registration's exit gate (§5) uses **multi-model adversarial review** —
   566	not a professional human audit, and not claimed as one. A substantive human
   567	audit requires a paid expert; anything less labelled "audit" would be theater. A
   568	real system has a *recurring* review cycle, not a one-time blessing. So review
   569	recency is made a verifiable property: the service attests *when it was last
   570	adversarially reviewed* (signed, timestamped), and review staleness beyond a
   571	declared threshold fails the invariant. The system that attests artifacts
   572	attests its own audit recency — dogfooding, not decoration.
   573	
   574	---
   575	
   576	## 5. Success criteria & kill criterion
   577	
   578	Three gates, in order, not to be conflated:
   579	
   580	- **The gate (§8 / H0)** comes first — the formal foundation. Nothing below
   581	  begins until it is resolved.
   582	- **The initial-slice gate (H1 §2)** — Band 1 frozen, then the LocalStack
   583	  vertical slice at 1/sec within 7 days of the declared Phase 1 start.
   584	- **The whole-project gate (§5 below)** — when Tessera is a credible **v1
   585	  product**, not merely an impressive demonstration. This is a deliberately
   586	  binary, checklist-able bar rather than a subjective "impressive enough"
   587	  judgment, because a credibility artifact wants a done-line a reader can check.
   588	
   589	**Done when (v1 product):**
   590	- Sustains **10,000 attestations in a 24-hour period** (~0.116/sec sustained).
   591	- Survives **multi-model adversarial review by at least three unrelated LLM
   592	  models**, including the web-facing surface. (This is the demonstration-project
   593	  adversarial-review mechanism — explicitly *not* a substitute for a professional
   594	  human security audit, and not labelled "audit.")
   595	- **Review recency is attested** (§4.7): the service publishes a signed,
   596	  timestamped record of when it was last adversarially reviewed, and treats
   597	  staleness past a declared threshold as a failing invariant — encoding that
   598	  review is a recurring cycle, not a one-time gate.
   599	- **Account lifecycle complete through sign-up and API-key management**
   600	  (creation, invalidation, usage-log access) — the security-relevant authn/authz
   601	  surface, red-bar tested (forge a key? use a revoked one? escalate via the
   602	  usage log?), not merely features. **Billing/payment is deferred** (a known
   603	  Stripe-shaped, SAQ-A-minimizing integration when paying demand is validated —
   604	  not built for v1).
   605	- **Architected to the PCI standard, not certified to it.** The cardholder-data
   606	  environment is minimized *by design* (tokenize via a compliant processor so
   607	  PCI scope is SAQ-A-shaped, not SAQ-D), evidencing segmentation, key-management,
   608	  and access-control judgment. Designing to the bar is the demonstration;
   609	  obtaining the certificate is deferred product work.
   610	- The scaling question — *if promoted for AI-agent use (e.g. openclaw,
   611	  hermes-agent), could it scale to handle success?* — is answered by
   612	  **architecture + load evidence** (a design-and-load-test claim, not a
   613	  production-traffic claim).
   614	
   615	**Kill criterion (further work is vanity):** the v1 done-line above is green and
   616	the scale-to-success path is demonstrated *on paper*. Past that point — tuning
   617	for traffic that does not yet exist, polishing UI, chasing scale that has not
   618	been sold — is vanity. Stop and ship the demonstration.
   619	
   620	---
   621	
   622	## 6. Method & division of labor (the meta-claim)
   623	
   624	The stance is contest-then-adopt: the prior design spec (a previous AI's work,
   625	in `docs/tessera-cloud-demo-spec.md`) is coherent and useful, but it is *not*
   626	ratified. Architecture decisions are re-derived or contested in the human
   627	author's voice; the disagreements are part of the credibility evidence, because
   628	a design merely executed from an AI's spec would demonstrate the deskilling
   629	failure mode, not the healthy one. AI accelerates mechanics; the judgment is
   630	the author's.
   631	
   632	**Disagreements with the prior spec, on the record:**
   633	
   634	1. **The prior spec's KMS-only signing fork (B) concentrates the trust story in
   635	   one operational domain.** KMS is technically capable (it signs Ed25519 and
   636	   even post-quantum ML-DSA, with offline-verifiable public keys) — so the
   637	   disagreement is *not* that KMS is incapable, as an earlier draft wrongly
   638	   implied. It is that resting a long-lived attestation solely on KMS ties its
   639	   verifiability to a single domain's account lifecycle, key-deletion policy,
   640	   IAM, and public-key provenance (§3.1). Rejected in favor of dual-signing (C)
   641	   across independent failure domains, with DNS/repo/bundle key publication and
   642	   a bundled issuer-key manifest. This was the load-bearing architectural
   643	   disagreement.
   644	
   645	2. **The prior spec under-specified supersession.** It had no model for adding
   646	   assurance (e.g. post-quantum) to existing attestations. The wrap-don't-
   647	   replace model (§3.1) — which also dissolves the canonicalization-freeze into
   648	   per-layer versioning — is a Tessera addition, not the spec's.
   649	
   650	3. **The prior spec treated the cloud-independent core and the LocalStack slice
   651	   as one phase.** They are separated here into bands (§2): the cryptographic
   652	   guarantees are frozen on a laptop *before* any LocalStack work, so the
   653	   cloud-shaped layers cannot silently regress the invariant.
   654	
   655	4. **The prior spec assumed the central guarantee, never demanded it be
   656	   proven.** It described an attestation service as if "tamper-evident,
   657	   cryptographically secure" were established by construction. It is not. The
   658	   guarantee that altering a signed package requires solving a hard problem is
   659	   an *unproven claim* until a machine-checked formal model and proof exist
   660	   (H0 / Band 0). Treating that as a gating precondition — building nothing
   661	   until the foundation is proven — is the largest divergence from the prior
   662	   spec, and the one most central to the difference between a commercial
   663	   offering and theater. Formal verification is itself a capability the project
   664	   demonstrates (§7).
   665	
   666	(Further disagreements are expected as the build proceeds; the journal records
   667	them as they arise.)
   668	
   669	*Right-sizing note (carried from the prior spec, and true): the documentation
   670	layer is a dated markdown journal, not an attestation system. The cleverness
   671	goes in the service, not the log.*
   672	
   673	---
   674	
   675	## 7. Capability-map rows targeted (the CV mechanics)
   676	
   677	The gap profile is coherent, not a random scatter, and it *is* the CV thesis
   678	made concrete: **I have the infrastructure primitives but not the operational,
   679	security-posture, and observability envelope around them.** I have built the
   680	components services are made of; the market wants people who can *operate*
   681	services. Tessera closes exactly that envelope. Stated honestly in three bands:
   682	
   683	**Direct gaps being closed (the point of the project — the "operate it" layer,
   684	mostly the SRE / security-engineering cluster, hard to fake and hard to learn
   685	from docs alone):**
   686	- ECS / Fargate
   687	- KMS (key custody)
   688	- CloudTrail (audit)
   689	- GuardDuty / Security Hub (threat detection / posture)
   690	- S3 Object Lock (WORM / immutability)
   691	- OpenTelemetry / Prometheus / Grafana (observability)
   692	- SLOs / error budgets / load testing / graceful degradation
   693	- CDK / Terraform IaC
   694	- FinOps / cost governance
   695	
   696	**Reinforced (already have hands-on; this project evidences depth, not novelty):**
   697	Lambda, SQS, DynamoDB, EC2, EBS, S3 (basic — *not* Object Lock), and security
   698	architecture generally.
   699	
   700	**Acknowledged stretch (a real edge of competence, named rather than hidden):**
   701	web-facing systems. I do not typically build public-facing web surfaces; the
   702	API gateway and (v1) web surface are a deliberate stretch, not a strength I am
   703	claiming. Declaring this edge is itself the point — the same "declare what you
   704	cannot see" ethos the attestation service embodies, applied to my own CV. A
   705	reviewer should trust the strong claims *more* because the weak edge is stated.
   706	
   707	---
   708	
   709	## 8. The gate — this project does not begin until these are true
   710	
   711	A pre-registration is only honest if it can name the condition under which the
   712	project *does not proceed*. Tessera has one, and it is non-negotiable:
   713	
   714	**Band 0 (H0) must be resolved before Phase 1 begins.** Concretely:
   715	- a machine-checked formal model (TLA+ and/or Lean4) of the attestation
   716	  construction and its claimed invariants exists, and the proof — resting on
   717	  cited external hardness assumptions, proving *our own composition* — holds;
   718	- the agreement gate has passed (author and AI each independently judge the
   719	  model faithful to the claim);
   720	- the informal written proof (the author's defend-it-cold account) is in the
   721	  repository.
   722	
   723	The gate is **machine-checked**, by design. Independent *human* formal-methods
   724	review is *not* a resolution requirement under the no-spend constraint (H3) — it
   725	is an optional enhancement gated on a revenue stream, and its absence is a
   726	declared residual risk (no review, machine or human, guarantees a future party
   727	won't find a hole). This keeps the bar rigorous *and* achievable in our control,
   728	rather than set where it can never be met.
   729	
   730	Only then does **Phase 1a** begin (implementing the cloud-independent crypto
   731	core), marked by an explicit, dated, signed commit declaring the build runway
   732	open. The 7-day H1b window opens later, at the H1a freeze commit (§2).
   733	
   734	**If H0 cannot be met, the project does not proceed as designed — and that is a
   735	valid, honest outcome of this pre-registration, not a failure to conceal.** A
   736	highlight reel cannot say "we stopped because the foundation did not hold." A
   737	real pre-registration can, and that is precisely where its credibility comes
   738	from. The central guarantee of an attestation service is not something to
   739	assert and build around; it is something to prove, or to honestly report we
   740	could not. Building on an unproven foundation would be theater — the one
   741	outcome this project exists to refute.

