   230	## A10.1 "Existed no later than T" is chain time, not UTC — OWNER DECISION, NARROW-CLAIM (review A01)
   231	
   232	A9 says the bytes and signatures "existed no later than a time T fixed by Bitcoin" (A9:36-37; again at :114,
   233	:133 and in C5, :144). A9 also says that header times are chain-time proxies (C5) and that a finite UTC cutoff
   234	"requires an additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish" (:444-446). The ordinary
   235	reading of "no later than T" is a real-time bound, which A9 does not support.
   236	
   237	**Reading adopted.** "No later than T" means chain order: the bytes and exact signatures existed before the
   238	selected Bitcoin block was mined, because that block commits to their SHA-256 hash (under the registered
   239	cryptographic and chain assumptions). T is that block's header time, a chain-time value. It is not an upper bound
   240	in UTC: A2.1 registers no finite bound on B − C, and neither ε nor S is one. A UTC reading needs the additional
   241	backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish. A10.1.1 recommends one such assumption, for a separate
   242	assessment only; this section does not depend on it.
   243	
   244	**Other registered uses of the same bound.** The scenario mapping (:177, :186), the issue order (:294-295) and
   245	guarantee 1 (:433-439) use T as the same clock bound. Each is qualified the same way (rows 5–9 of A10.7). These
   246	qualifications change the explanation of the safe decisions, not their verdicts, fixtures or case membership.
   247	Scenario i's parties' signature-checking duty and guarantee 1's `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED` stay. The
   248	issue-order case is concrete: a signature made after a backward-skewed header's nominal UTC time, but before the
   249	block is mined, can fit the block's anchor.
   250	
   251	**Assembled C5 claim cell** (rows 4 and 17 of A10.7 both point here):
   252	
   253	> The frames **and their exact signatures** existed before the selected Bitcoin block was mined. T is that block's
   254	> header time, not a UTC upper bound. The selected block belongs to the qualifying Bitcoin attestation at the lowest
   255	> block height in the presented OTS proof, under A10.5's stages; T and its confirmations come from that same
   256	> attestation. The three-conjunct predicate holds at k = 6. Header times need not be monotone; selection uses chain
   257	> order, not the least header time (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14, F03).
   258	
   259	C5's non-claims stay, with the reorganization residual amended by A10.3 (row 14). Under A10.1.1 (author adopted
   260	2026-10-18), row 21 adds its conditional sentence after this cell; the cell itself does not change.
