    96	## 2. F2: accepted signature encodings and rule-O policy
    97	
    98	**Rule O encoding (gpg slot; fallback kms slot).** Accepted: exactly one legacy-format signature packet, `0x88 u8`
    99	(body ≤ 255) or `0x89 u16` (256..1021), with a v4 body of type 0x00, algorithm 19, hash 8, shortest-form subpackets,
   100	two prefix octets, then MPIs r and s of 1..256 bits each with exact bit counts; r, s ∈ [1, n−1].
   101	
   102	- **Both low-S and high-S are accepted.** O-01 is low-S (s ≤ n/2). O-02 is its high-S twin (r, n−s). A test asserts
   103	  which is which (`test_O01_is_low_s_and_O02_is_its_high_s_twin`).
   104	- **DER is never an accepted input form.** The verifier builds the DER itself from the parsed MPIs. The analogue of
   105	  non-canonical DER, a non-minimal MPI, is `SIG_FORMAT` (O-15).
   106	
   107	| Rejected | Code | Vectors |
   108	|---|---|---|
   109	| OpenPGP-format header, non-shortest header, bare DER, raw r‖s, armor | `SIG_PACKET` | O-04..O-08 |
   110	| trailing / truncated | `TRAILING` / `TRUNCATED` | O-09, O-10 |
   111	| v5, type 0x01, algorithm 22, hash 10, MPI with leading zero, 257-bit MPI, r = 0 | `SIG_FORMAT` | O-11..O-17 |
   112	| r = n, s = n, r = 2²⁵⁶−1 | `SIG_POLICY` | O-18..O-20 |
   113	| prefix octets wrong, other document, other key | `SIG_INVALID` | O-21..O-23 |
   114	| rule-O packet in the hybrid kms slot | `SIG_FORMAT` | O-25 |
   115	
   116	**Rule-O signature policy** (V1-PROFILE §8, "Rule-O signature policy" and "Advisory metadata, structural
   117	validation"; `codec.sig_policy`). Every P vector is a correctly signed packet over its own hashed area.
   118	`TestRuleOPolicy::test_policy_vector_is_correctly_signed` checks that each one verifies when `sig_policy` is
   119	skipped, so each `SIG_POLICY` outcome is the policy's doing.
   120	
   121	- **Hashed area:**
   122	  - exactly one creation-time subpacket (type 2) with exactly 4 data octets, critical or not;
   123	  - no signature-expiration subpacket (type 3), critical or not;
   124	  - types 16 and 33 accepted, critical or not, with exact structure: at most one 16, of 8 octets; at most one 33, of
   125	    21 octets with first octet 04; if both are present, 16 equals the last 8 octets of 33;
   126	  - any other type is ignored if noncritical and a failure if critical.
   127	- **Unhashed area:** ignored entirely, critical bits included, after ENC's subpacket framing.
   128	
   129	| Case | Outcome | Vectors |
   130	|---|---|---|
   131	| creation time critical; GnuPG shape (hashed 2 + 33, unhashed 16); hashed 16 + 33 consistent; 16 + 33 both critical; unknown noncritical hashed type | `ACCEPT` | P-01..P-05 |
   132	| unhashed area holding a critical unknown, a critical expiration, malformed and inconsistent 16/33, and duplicate creation times | `ACCEPT` (ignored) | P-06 |
   133	| creation time missing, duplicated, 3, 5 or 0 data octets | `SIG_POLICY` | P-10..P-14 |
   134	| expiration, noncritical or critical | `SIG_POLICY` | P-15, P-16 |
   135	| unknown critical type; RFC-known type 4 marked critical | `SIG_POLICY` | P-17, P-18 |
   136	| type 16 of 7 octets; two type 16; type 33 of 20 octets; type 33 version 05; two type 33; 16/33 inconsistent; zero-data critical 33 | `SIG_POLICY` | P-19..P-25 |
   137	
   138	**Rule R (hybrid kms slot).** Accepted: exactly 3309 bytes that pass the FIPS 204 HintBitUnpack and z-bound
   139	conditions and verify under pure ML-DSA-65 with the empty context. Positive controls: a library `sign(F, context=b'')`
   140	signature (R-01) and an `EXTERNAL_MU`-style `sign_mu` signature (R-02, the issuer's path).
   141	
   142	| Rejected | Code | Vectors |
   143	|---|---|---|
   144	| length 0 | `SIG_EMPTY` | R-03 |
   145	| length 3308, ML-DSA-44 (2420) | `SIG_FORMAT` | R-04, R-06 |
   146	| length 3310, ML-DSA-87 (4627) | `LENGTH_CAP` | R-05, R-07 |
   147	| hint: nonzero padding, duplicate index, swapped indices (each decodes to the **same** h under a lax decoder), count ω+1, decreasing counts | `SIG_INVALID` | R-08..R-12 |
   148	| z coefficient = ±(γ1−β) | `SIG_INVALID` | R-13, R-14 |
   149	| z in range but wrong, c̃ flipped, other document, other key | `SIG_INVALID` | R-15..R-18 |
   150	
   151	R-08..R-14 are rejected by the verifier's own gate before the library is called. `TestMLDSAGate` proves this with a
   152	permissive mock backend, so rejection does not depend on library strictness. The library also rejects them without
   153	the gate (LIB07), so with this build the gate is defence in depth.
   154	
   155	## 3. F3: public-key validation behaviour
   156	
   157	**P-256 (gpg packet; fallback kms SPKI; repository key).** The verifier requires:
   158	
   159	- the exact encoding: the 82-byte v4 packet body with prefix `13 08 2a8648ce3d030107 0203`, or the 26-byte SPKI prefix
   160	  plus 65 bytes;
   161	- an **uncompressed** point `04‖X‖Y`.
   162	
   163	It then checks **X < p, Y < p and the curve equation itself, before the EC constructor is called**. All of the
   164	following are `KEY_FORMAT`:
   165	
   166	| Case | Vectors |
   167	|---|---|
   168	| compressed point | K-05, K-17 |
   169	| hybrid 06/07 encoding | K-06 |
   170	| off-curve point | K-03, K-15 |
   171	| non-canonical X = x+p | K-04, K-16 |
   172	| non-canonical Y = y+p | K-36, K-37 |
   173	| other curves | K-09, K-18, K-19 |
   174	| wrong version, algorithm, MPI count or length | K-07, K-08, K-10..K-12 |
   175	| cross-slot encodings | K-13, K-20, K-21 |
   176	
   177	A bad repository key refuses the trust configuration (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, B07).
   178	
   179	**Ordering, tested (P3R-02).** `TestPointBeforeConstructor` replaces `codec.ec`, for the codec module only, with a
   180	permissive stand-in whose constructor accepts anything:
   181	
   182	- **Rejected without the constructor ever being called:** X ≥ p (K-04, K-16), Y ≥ p (K-36, K-37), curve membership
   183	  (K-03, K-15, L-03, L-01), and representation (compressed, hybrid, leading 00 or 05).
   184	- **Positive controls reach the constructor with the exact (x, y):** K-01, K-02, K-35, K-14.
   185	
   186	So the verifier's own checks decide these cases, not the library's defaults. This matters because the library alone
   187	**accepts X ≥ p and Y ≥ p and reduces them** (LIB02, LIB09). Its DER loader also **accepts compressed-point SPKIs and
   188	other curves** (LIB03). The library does reject off-curve points by itself (LIB01), so for curve membership the
   189	verifier's check is shown, by the mock, to come first, but is redundant with this build.
   190	
   191	**ML-DSA-65 (hybrid kms).** The only check is the exact 1974-byte SPKI: OID 2.16.840.1.101.3.4.3.18, parameters
   192	absent, zero unused bits, minimal DER (K-22 accepted; K-24..K-34 `KEY_FORMAT`). There is no further public-key
   193	validation. Every 1952-byte string is a well-formed FIPS 204 key: the all-zero key is accepted (K-23), and the
   194	library accepts it too (LIB06). That is decoding behaviour, not a security claim about such a key. The library's
   195	generic DER loader would accept ML-DSA-44/-87 SPKIs as keys of their own parameter sets (LIB04); the verifier's
   196	exact-prefix rule rejects them (K-28, K-29).
   197	
   198	## 4. Translation of A1's Ed25519 fields — OWNER READING, contestable
   199	
   200	**Not decided here: the algorithm policy.** V1 *requires* both algorithms. The frozen catalog names only the hybrid
   201	`KMS-MLDSA65-PURE-GPG-OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (registered 2026-10-16). Any other algorithm is `ALG_UNKNOWN`
   202	(`verify.py:180`), and a receipt missing either signature fails (G3). This section decides only whether checking each
   203	of the two algorithms against its own analogue of A1's Ed25519 checklist meets P3's registered words. Because both
   204	signatures are required, a forgery must break both. A weakness noted below for one algorithm matters only if the
   205	other algorithm also fails.
   206	
   207	The registered text names Ed25519 concepts. V1 does not verify Ed25519. The mapping below is the owner's reading and
   208	is open to challenge: whether these analogues discharge the registered words is for the author to rule. (Codex
   209	review: "a reasonable, explicitly contestable owner reading, not a concealed amendment"; its sufficiency still
   210	needs the author's ruling.)
   211	
   212	| A1 field (Ed25519 sense) | V1 analogue: ECDSA P-256 (rule O) | V1 analogue: ML-DSA-65 (rule R) |
   213	|---|---|---|
   214	| Exact library and version | §1 | §1 |
   215	| Accepted signature encodings (canonical S < L, canonical R) | §2: OpenPGP v4 packet, exact-bit-count MPIs, r, s ∈ [1, n−1], rule-O policy. **High-S accepted**: ECDSA's (r, s)/(r, n−s) malleability is not removed, and the profile relies on the anchor's exact-copy commitment (ENC §3.5) | §2: exact 3309 bytes; strict HintBitUnpack and z bound, so one (c̃, z, h) has one encoding (R-08..R-10) |
   216	| Public-key validation (canonical A encoding, small-order A) | §3: exact uncompressed encoding, coordinates < p, on-curve, all before the constructor | §3: exact SPKI only. **No algebraic validation exists**, so this is "not applicable beyond encoding" |
   217	| Low-order-point handling (the 8-torsion of Curve25519) | P-256 has **cofactor h = 1**: every finite on-curve point has prime order n, and the only low-order element is the point at infinity. As a **public key** it cannot be received (L-01, L-02, L-06, L-08 `KEY_FORMAT`); as the **result of the verification equation** it is rejected (E-11). The live residue of the concern is **invalid-curve points**, including small-order points on other curves with the same a; the verifier's on-curve check rejects them before the constructor (L-03, an order-2 point of y² = x³ − 3x + 2; L-04, L-07). h = 1 is a property of the curve (SP 800-186), not something a test here derives | **Not applicable**: ML-DSA is lattice-based, with no group, cofactor or torsion. Degenerate keys (e.g. all-zero t1) are accepted (K-23); whether they weaken anything is not tested here |
   218	| Cofactor equation variant (cofactored [8]SB = [8]R + [8]kA vs cofactorless) | With h = 1 the cofactored/cofactorless distinction does not exist. The equation choices that remain, all tested: the message representative is SHA256(F ‖ v4 trailer), passed `Prehashed`, with no second hash and no plain SHA256(F) (E-01..E-03); the hash prefix is checked; **x(R) is reduced mod n before comparison with r**: E-10 is a constructed key with x(R) = n + 3, r = 3, s = 1, accepted, where an unreduced comparison would reject (`TestEquationConstruction` confirms x(R) ≥ n; LIB10 shows the library does the reduction); a verification result of infinity is rejected (E-11) | The variant question becomes **which FIPS 204 interface**. The verifier uses pure ML-DSA, empty context: M′ = 00 ‖ 00 ‖ F. A non-empty context (E-04, E-09), HashML-DSA (E-05, E-06), the internal interface with no domain prefix (E-07) and μ passed as the message (E-08) are all `SIG_INVALID` |
   219	| (P3's premise) the verification equation is not issuer binding | **Demonstrated**: from (F, S) alone, `p256.dsks_keys` computes a different valid P-256 key under which the honest signature verifies (D-02 `ACCEPT` at the function level). In a bundle that key is rejected by the signed fingerprint binding: `KEY_MISMATCH` (B02). Rewriting the manifest to match gives `RECEIPT_MANIFEST_MISMATCH` (B03). This concerns substitution into existing honest signed material, not receipts minted by an attacker who holds both channels | Not demonstrated either way. ML-DSA hashes tr = H(pk) into μ, which is the usual argument for its exclusive-ownership properties; no test here exercises it |
   220	
   221	E-10 and E-11 follow Codex's construction (review scratch `probe.py`): choose an on-curve R with x(R) = n + r, then
   222	Q = r⁻¹(R − eG) with s = 1. For E-11, Q = (−e/r)G, so eG + rQ = O.
   223	
   224	## 5. Library behaviour claims and the test behind each
   225	
