    76	---
    77	
    78	## A2.1 "Confirmed within δ": one predicate, both sides
    79	
    80	**Vocabulary.** The anchor transaction is included in the block at height
    81	`h`. Confirmation count follows the Bitcoin convention: the including
    82	block is the first confirmation, so `c = tip_height − h + 1`, and the
    83	block that grants the k-th confirmation has height `h + k − 1`. Define
    84	
    85	    confirmed_at  :=  timestamp(block at height h + k − 1)
    86	
    87	**The rule.** Issuance is complete only if
    88	
    89	    confirmed_at  ≤  declared_issue_time + δ
    90	
    91	The issuer evaluates this conjunct **as part of the full three-conjunct
    92	temporal predicate** (the P5 temporal test inside `VALID_STRICT`; all
    93	three A2.2 conjuncts — not the whole verdict, which contains more than
    94	the temporal test) before shipping: under
    95	skewed, non-monotonic block timestamps the confirmation conjunct does
    96	not imply the anchor-time conjunct, so shipping on this conjunct alone
    97	could ship a receipt the verifier rejects (surfaced by the bridge model,
    98	`formal/tla/P5cP5P6_Bridge.tla`, 2026-07-21). The verifier evaluates the
    99	same conjuncts on the same chain observables at verification (A2.2),
   100	**under the issuance policy's tolerances**: the verifier-owns-tolerances
   101	discipline permits stricter verifier values of either tolerance (a
   102	stricter δ′ < δ, or a stricter ε′ on the anchor-time lower bound), and
   103	such a verifier may reject honestly-shipped receipts — the
   104	no-disagreement guarantee below is scoped to verification under the
   105	issuance policy's δ and ε.
   106	**Clock roles (ruling, 2026-07-21).** Chain time and wall time hold
   107	separate, explicitly-ranked roles. **Chain time governs evidence:** the
   108	three-conjunct predicate above, evaluated identically by issuer and
   109	verifier on the designated blocks' timestamps. **Wall time governs the
   110	attempt lifecycle:** the service waits through `declared + δ + S` before
   111	treating an attempt as expired, where S ≥ 0 is an **operational slack
   112	constant** joining δ, ε, k, N for ratification at Band 0 exit (working
   113	default S = 24 h, sized to the observation path — outages and polling,
   114	not timestamp skew alone). **No global bound on the backward observation
   115	lag is assumed.** Writing C = `confirmed_at` and B = the service's
   116	wall-clock observation of the designated block: Bitcoin consensus
   117	provides no finite bound on B − C (median-time-past and the two-hour
   118	future rule bound the forward direction only), and B additionally
   119	absorbs the entire observation path — network and header-store delay,
   120	polling, outage and recovery, wall-clock error. Registering a hard
   121	bound would assert, at Phase 0, a property of an operational stack that
   122	does not yet exist. Instead the guarantee is registered **conditionally
   123	on the observable antecedent**: *if B − C ≤ S for an attempt whose
   124	predicate holds, that attempt has a live shipping opportunity at
   125	eligibility* (slack-parameter analysis,
   126	`formal/tla/P5cP5P6_BridgeSlack.tla` and its `_Latch` variant;
   127	predictions-first record in
   128	`docs/reviews/2026-07-21-claude-predictions-slack-bench.md`). Since B
   129	and C are both observable, a violated antecedent is a detectable
   130	operating-envelope event, not a silently failed assumption. Timeliness
   131	is **latched at eligibility**: an attempt eligible within the envelope
   132	cannot be expired by later packaging or scheduling delay —
   133	eligible/finalizing, shipped, and expired/refused are mutually
   134	exclusive states (a construction obligation on the implementation,
   135	registered with the ruling). An attempt whose lag exceeds S may be
   136	refused; a chain-valid artifact of such an attempt remains
   137	cryptographically valid and evidentially admissible but carries **no
   138	protocol standing** (A2.4; enforcing that distinction against later
   139	publication is Amendment 3's lineage/standing obligation). If
   140	operational evidence later supports an SLA-style hard bound, it may
   141	enter a **deployment profile** with monitoring and an explicit
   142	violation outcome — never this pre-registration's assumption set. What
   143	the opportunity guarantee buys is opportunity, not outcome: "every
   144	timely-eligible attempt ships" is a contract obligation on the
   145	implementation, the same proof-versus-contract split as A2.3's refusal.
   146	
   147	**Boundary ties, registered per clock.** The chain-side predicate is
   148	stateless: at exactly `confirmed_at = declared + δ` it holds, and holds
   149	identically whenever evaluated — there is no chain-side race. The
   150	wall-side boundary is **resolved by the latch, not raced** (round-3
   151	repair, 2026-07-21: an earlier draft of this paragraph carried the
   152	fused-clock race forward; under latched eligibility there is none).
   153	Eligibility observed at exactly `B = declared + δ + S` is timely — the
   154	boundary is **inclusive and latch-winning**, matching the mechanized
   155	form (`burialAtWall <= declared + Delta + Slack` in the `_Latch`
   156	companion) — so such an attempt latches eligible/finalizing and cannot
   157	expire. Expiry (on the final attempt, refusal) applies only **strictly
   158	after** the boundary, and only to attempts with no timely-latched
   159	eligibility. Atomicity of the latch against the expiry transition is
   160	part of the mutual-exclusion construction obligation (A2.3). If the
   161	predicate fails at issuance time, the attempt is discarded and
   162	re-issued per the P5 corollary, subject to the attempt bound of A2.3.
   163	
   164	**Why chain time on both sides.** A wall-clock ship rule ("ship if depth k
   165	by `now ≤ declared + δ`") and a block-timestamp verifier check can
   166	disagree at the δ boundary by the consensus-bounded skew (block timestamps
   167	may run up to about two hours ahead of network-adjusted time). Defining
   168	the rule once, on the chain-visible quantity, removes the divergence: **no
   169	honestly-shipped receipt can fail the verifier's confirmation conjunct
   170	under the issuance policy's δ**, and the issuer's ship rule and the
   171	verifier predicate become the same intended statement about the same
   172	observable under the declared abstractions — the correspondence A1.4
   173	demands, asserted here and checked mechanically by the bridge model
   174	(`formal/tla/P5cP5P6_Bridge.tla`; its own non-author review pending).
   175	P5c's fused-clock guard (`now ≤ declared + δ` at depth k) is a locally
   176	*stronger* condition that implies the chain predicate under that
   177	module's single-clock abstraction; with the clocks decoupled, the
   178	bridge's `Ship` evaluates the chain quantities directly.
   179	
   180	**Named residuals.**
   181	
   182	- *Adversarial timestamping.* A miner can set `timestamp(h + k − 1)`
   183	  forward within the consensus bound, pushing an honest issuance into the
   184	  tail case. This is a griefing surface bounded to hours against δ = 72
   185	  hours; it can force a re-issue, never a false verdict.
   186	- *Non-monotonic timestamps.* Block timestamps are not monotonic;
   187	  `confirmed_at` may even precede `anchor_time`. The predicate is a single
   188	  comparison on one designated block and is well-defined regardless; no
   189	  monotonicity is assumed.
   190	- *`confirmed_at` is a proxy, including backward skew.* The observable is
   191	  the designated block's header timestamp — the chosen chain-time proxy
   192	  for burial, not a measurement of when depth k was reached in wall-clock
   193	  time. Median-time-past rules allow a block mined after a real-time
   194	  deadline to carry an in-window timestamp (the mirror of the
   195	  forward-skew griefing above). The predicate is well-defined on the
   196	  proxy; wall-clock burial lateness is not what it measures.
   197	- *Header provenance.* The conjunct's verdict is only as trustworthy as
   198	  the headers it is evaluated over. Bundled headers form a candidate
   199	  chain segment, not proof of canonicity: a privately-mined in-window
   200	  fork presented in a bundle, or a lying header store, yields false
   201	  acceptance under naive stateless evaluation. Header authentication
   202	  against the canonical chain — proof-of-work validity, cumulative-work
   203	  or checkpoint anchoring, store identity pinned in declared verifier
   204	  policy, bundle/store conflict rules — is part of the A2.2 evidence
   205	  obligation; its full specification is deferred to that obligation's
   206	  discharge, not silently assumed here.
   207	
   208	**Model-convention pin (off-by-one, fixed here so it cannot drift).**
   209	P5c's `depth` variable counts blocks mined *after* inclusion (`depth = 0`
   210	at inclusion), so `depth ≥ DepthK` corresponds to `c ≥ DepthK + 1`. The
   211	correspondence mapping instantiates **`DepthK = k − 1`** (for the strict
   212	default k = 6: `DepthK = 5`). δ, ε, k remain ratified (or revised, on the
   213	record) at Band 0 exit, as registered; δ's 72-hour sizing already included
   214	aggregation-to-confirmation lag, so this amendment does not move it.
   215	
   216	---
   217	
   218	## A2.2 P5, amended: the strict temporal check gains a third conjunct
   219	
   220	`VALID_STRICT` now requires all of:
   221	
   222	    declared_issue_time − ε  ≤  anchor_time                       (unchanged)
   223	    anchor_time              ≤  declared_issue_time + δ           (unchanged)
   224	    confirmed_at             ≤  declared_issue_time + δ           (new)
   225	
   226	Consequences, stated explicitly:
   227	
   228	- The **chain-late subclass** of the A2.0 abandoned-anchor artifact
   229	  (`confirmed_at > declared + δ`) is **rejected outright** — its
   230	  `confirmed_at` exceeds its own window. Within this system the conjunct
   231	  rejects *only* such artifacts: strict issuance ships nothing that fails
   232	  it (A2.1). An operationally abandoned but chain-valid artifact passes
   233	  this conjunct and is governed instead by the A2.1/A2.4 standing rules
   234	  (no protocol standing; closure of the two-receipts residue is
   235	  Amendment 3's obligation, per A2.0).
   236	- **Evidence obligation.** The verifier must evaluate `confirmed_at`
   237	  statelessly (P9): block headers `h … h + k − 1` are available either
   238	  archived in the bundle or from the verifier-distributed header store the
   239	  anchor check already requires — the same trust-configuration pattern as
   240	  A1.5's historical trust-anchor store. Header *authentication* rules
   241	  (canonicity, work validation, store identity in declared policy,
   242	  bundle/store conflict resolution — the header-provenance residual of
   243	  A2.1) are part of this obligation. This joins the H1a evidence
   244	  obligations.
   245	- **Waiver status.** Temporal-anchor consistency is already non-waivable
   246	  (A1.2.1); the new conjunct is part of it. The P4 partition applies
   247	  inside it: performed-and-failed → `INVALID`; unperformable (headers
   248	  unavailable) → `UNVERIFIABLE`; neither is promotable.
   249	
   250	---
   251	
   252	## A2.3 The re-issue loop terminates: bounded attempts, fail-closed refusal
   253	
   254	As registered, the P5 corollary's re-issue rule was an unbounded loop, and
   255	its dominant triggers — fee spikes, congestion, calendar outage — are
   256	*correlated across attempts*: retrying under the same conditions is not a
   257	fresh draw, so "re-issue until it works" can livelock. Fail-closed means
   258	issuance must be able to end in refusal, not only in success.
   259	
   260	Amended: issuance makes at most **N attempts**. The expiry clock is the
   261	**wall clock** (A2.1 clock roles): an attempt expires when wall time
   262	passes `declared + δ + S`, except that timely-latched eligibility
   263	excludes expiry — eligible/finalizing, shipped, and expired/refused are
   264	mutually exclusive states (construction obligation, registered with the
   265	ruling). Exhausting them obligates
   266	the implementation to terminate issuance in an **explicit refusal, durably
   267	recorded; reporting is a delivery obligation to be registered in
   268	Amendment 3** — a first-class protocol outcome, not an error path. N is a
   269	protocol constant ratified at Band 0 exit alongside δ, ε, k, and S
   270	(working default **N = 3**; per the A2.1 clock-roles ruling each
   271	attempt's lifecycle is wall-governed through δ + S, so N attempts give
   272	a **nominal lifecycle budget** of N × (δ + S) — twelve days at the
   273	working defaults δ = 72 h, S = 24 h — a nominal budget, not a proven
   274	wall-clock bound on the process; see the model note).
   275	[**Note:** refusal as a separate delivery obligation added 2026/07/21 per
   276	review process/response.]
   277	
   278	Model note: P5c's `MaxAttempts` is hereby promoted from a state-space
   279	bound to protocol semantics. The refusal state is modeled by **atomic
   280	entry** (the transition expiring the final attempt's window records the
   281	refusal in the same step; checked, with a broken companion red on
   282	exactly the silent-deadlock invariant among the checked set — review
   283	archived in
   284	`docs/reviews/2026-07-20-codex-p5c-refusal-review.md`). Scope of proof
   285	versus contract, per that review: the model proves the *conditional
   286	safety half* — the refusal state is entered atomically and latches; it
   287	does not prove the crossing occurs (no fairness is assumed), so
   288	**termination is a contract obligation on the implementation, not a
   289	proven liveness property**. The unbounded "eventually ships" claim
   290	likewise remains explicitly unproven. Storage durability, retrievability,
   291	and reporting of the refusal record are implementation/handoff
   292	obligations to be registered in Amendment 3 (planned, not yet in force).
   293	[**Note:** model-note scope split added 2026/07/21, same review cycle.]
   294	
   295	---
   296	
   297	## A2.4 Anchor identity and declared-time confinement
   298	
   299	- **A receipt's anchor is the proof it ships with.** A shipped receipt
   300	  contains exactly one anchor proof (the OTS proof over its canonical
   301	  bytes) together with the anchor transaction's id, which serves as the
   302	  **identity handle** binding the receipt to its chain transaction. (An
   303	  identity/coherence handle, not by itself a retrieval mechanism:
   304	  txid → transaction *availability* requires chain access — a node,
   305	  index, or archive — which is an implementation obligation under the
   306	  evidence discipline, joining A2.2's; the hash argument below covers
   307	  identity, not availability.) Verification evaluates the
   308	  shipped proof and no other; an anchor not shipped inside a receipt
   309	  confers **no validity, no priority, and no claimed anchor identity**
   310	  in verification or dispute — in particular, a discarded attempt's
   311	  transaction confirming later confers nothing toward any of the three.
   312	  (This confines *protocol standing*; it does not render such artifacts
   313	  inadmissible as *evidence* — e.g., of issuer conduct — in dispute
   314	  processes. The stand-alone anchor-standing property is Amendment 3's
   315	  to register; this clause is written to underdetermine it, not
   316	  pre-empt it.) The handle
   317	  requires no new assumption: a txid is the double-SHA-256 of the
   318	  transaction, so txid → transaction → committed bytes rests on the
