   113	Amendment 1 (line 212) says: "Revocation effective *after*
   114	`anchor_time` does not retroactively change the verdict." The
   115	registered two-conjunct rule two lines earlier requires authorization
   116	at `declared_issue_time` **and** no revocation at or before
   117	`anchor_time`; when the anchor precedes the declared time (possible
   118	within ε), a revocation between them invalidates, and
   119	`AuthorizedAtDeclared` in `P5P6_TemporalRevocation.tla` correctly
   120	says so. The sentence is hereby written: "Revocation effective after
   121	both `anchor_time` and `declared_issue_time` does not retroactively
   122	change the verdict." The author: *"fix the prose to conform with the
   123	intent."*
   124	
   125	## A4.4 The gate's third clause: what the written proof is
   126	
   127	`phase-0-prereg.md` §8 requires, before Phase 1a, "the informal
   128	written proof (the author's defend-it-cold account) is in the
   129	repository." Under the author's 2026-09-06 ruling on the scope of
   130	"defend it cold" (the project, its core decisions, and what it can
   131	and cannot do — never per-proof tool fluency) and the suite's
   132	amendment note 5, that account **is the collection of each checked
   133	model's plain-language statement of claim, adversary, and boundary**,
   134	carried with the model as a reading aid. The artifact must exist for
   135	exit. Its sufficiency for non-expert readers is *reviewed* (by the
   136	lower-ceiling reader probe and by the blind reverse-translation) and
   137	is **not** a gate; the correctness gate for each model is cross-family
   138	falsification review. The author: *"I'm trying to not block this
   139	project as much as possible."* The second clause of §8 (author and AI
   140	each judge the model faithful to the claim) is satisfied from that
   141	statement and the cross-family comparator, which this amendment states
   142	so that no reader infers the author read the tool text cold.
   143	
   144	## A4.5 P7: two signed-object types added
   145	
   146	P7's enumerated set — base attestation, wrapper, issuer-key manifest,
   147	authority evidence, conformance vector, review-recency attestation —
   148	gains two members: the **terminal lineage record** (the standing-
   149	evidence construction adopted 2026-08-31, entered 2026-09-04) and the
   150	**portable refusal record** (A3 §A3.7.2). Each carries its own
   151	domain-separation tag inside its signed bytes, and no trace may accept
   152	an object of either type as another. Until this amendment is signed
   153	the S-STANDING plan declares the TLR tag model-local; on signature the
   154	tags enter the shared theory library as a recorded divergence.
   155	
   156	## A4.6 Degraded mode: the general principle — RULED (author)
   157	
   158	Applied first to P3 (2026-09-05, S-P3 F8), then to standing
   159	(S-STANDING plan, 2026-09-06), and now registered once so it is not
   160	re-derived per property:
   161	
   162	> In degraded mode — any verdict other than `VALID_STRICT`, including
   163	> the sole-channel-compromised case — the verifier's job is to hand
   164	> the adjudicator the evidence of what could and could not be
   165	> excluded, marked as degraded per §A1.2.1's explicit-policy logic.
   166	> Tessera does not decide whether the adjudicator should trust the
   167	> attestation and must not try to. A correspondence that holds in
   168	> strict mode and fails in degraded mode is therefore a registered
   169	> **cost** of degradation, to be stated in the relying-party story,
   170	> never a defect of the construction — provided the verdict says it is
   171	> degraded.
   172	
   173	The author's reason, in his words: *"In 2106, Tessera won't exist in
   174	its current form and the only honest story is the adjudicator will
   175	have to make the decisions based upon the information available to
   176	them; anything else would imply some long-term service guarantees of
   177	Tessera, which I view as dishonest."* Vocabulary registered with it:
   178	the **verifier** is P9's pure function (bundle and declared policy
   179	in, verdict out); the **adjudicator** is the relying party who decides
   180	trust using the verdict and anything else available, including any
   181	third-party log (band-1 docket item 27). An enumeration or
   182	observability API may feed a log and may never feed a verdict.
