    93	## A5.2 Waived-but-failing evidence: the registered reading — ADOPTED (author)
    94	
    95	**The fork the P10 probe exposed** (`formal/spike/tla-probes/P10/RESULTS-PROBE.md`
    96	R1 and §C3). A `VALID_DEGRADED` verdict can coexist with a waived
    97	channel evidence that is present and fails — invalid signature, late
    98	anchor, or naming a different key. Under P4 as ratified (A4.2; waived
    99	checks do not determine the verdict) the verdict stands and the
   100	observation goes in the record. Under A3 §A3.2 item 2's second
   101	sentence read literally — "Evidence that is present and whose
   102	validation is performed but fails yields `INVALID`" — the verifier
   103	refuses. Both probe modules pass every invariant; they differ on the
   104	classification of that state, and the literal reading turns some
   105	`VALID_DEGRADED` and `UNVERIFIABLE` cases into `INVALID`. Its cost is
   106	an **availability attack**: an adversary who alters only the waived
   107	evidence in a bundle — no authority compromised, bytes it may alter
   108	under A1.3 item 1 — can turn an otherwise `VALID_DEGRADED` package
   109	into `INVALID`.
   110	
   111	**Registered reading: P4's.** A3 §A3.2 item 2's second sentence is
   112	hereby written: "**Required** evidence that is present and whose
   113	validation is performed but fails yields `INVALID`." Evidence the
   114	declared policy has waived is evaluated and its condition recorded
   115	(§A5.3) and never determines the verdict. Reasons: A3.2 item 5
   116	already says P4 "remains correct but incomplete by abstraction";
   117	refusing on evidence the policy waived contradicts the waiver's
   118	purpose; and the alternative hands an availability attack to anyone
   119	who can touch the bundle. The P10 probe's `_A32Reading` module is
   120	the recorded rejected alternative. P4's `ExactDegraded` invariant is
   121	unchanged.
   122	
   123	**The registered reading's own cost, recorded.** It is not free: a
   124	package may be reported `VALID_DEGRADED` while its bundle carries
   125	waived evidence that is present and demonstrably fails — including one
   126	naming a different key, which is evidence of equivocation and not mere
   127	absence. The registered mitigation is §A5.3 (the observation is
   128	evaluated and recorded) read with §A4.6 as bounded by §A5.7 (the
   129	degraded label excuses nothing the waiver rules did not permit, and
   130	the final evidence chain is not waivable — A3 §A3.2 item 1). That cost
   131	belongs in the relying-party story, stated as the counterpart of the
   132	availability attack the rejected reading carried.
   133	
   134	## A5.3 What a degraded verdict records — ADOPTED (author)
   135	
   136	**Two different "why"s.** P10 says the degraded verdict names "which
   137	authority evidence was waived and why"; §A1.2.1 says the record holds
   138	"the precise waived check set and the policy that authorized the
   139	waiver". The first is what the verifier *observed* about the waived
   140	evidence; the second is the *policy* that permitted the waiver. No
   141	registered text required the observation to be recorded; under §A4.6
   142	it is the load-bearing half for an adjudicator, who otherwise cannot
   143	distinguish a lapsed registrar from a conflicting publication.
   144	
   145	**Registered: both.** The verifier evaluates what it does not
   146	require, and the record holds, for each waived check, the policy that
   147	authorized the waiver **and** the verifier's observation of the
   148	waived evidence — at least the six values the probe needed: absent,
   149	unperformable, invalid, anchor-late, names-other-key, would-pass.
   150	Evaluation is bounded by the bundle and the declared inputs (P9);
   151	inability to evaluate is itself one of the recorded observations and
   152	never a reason to consult anything. The record format is an H1a
   153	obligation, entered on the band-1 docket. (ROUTED C4, with Codex's two
   154	refinements.)
