   221	## A5.7 The boundary of §A4.6 — ADOPTED (author)
   222	
   223	Amendment 4 §A4.6 says a correspondence that holds in strict mode and
   224	fails in degraded mode is "never a defect of the construction —
   225	provided the verdict says it is degraded." That is broader than what
   226	the author ruled: read literally it would excuse accepting altered
   227	bytes under an honest key (a P1 violation) or losing the last
   228	complete evidence chain (an A3.2 floor violation) so long as the
   229	verdict said "degraded". The author's reason — Tessera does not
   230	decide for the adjudicator — licenses neither. §A4.6 is hereby
   231	bounded by the following sentence, Codex's wording, adopted verbatim:
   232	
   233	> Only losses permitted by the registered waiver rules and the
   234	> declared policy are degradation costs. A degraded label does not
   235	> excuse violating a non-waivable requirement or misreporting a
   236	> required check's outcome.
   237	
   238	This keeps the verifier/adjudicator division, bounds what Tessera
   239	claims to have verified, and explicitly prevents a declared policy
   240	from waiving the final evidence chain. The drafting error was the
   241	collaborator's; the record of its detection is the Codex pre-read.
